{"packet_id":"D067-01","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey Security Deposit Law cap and return rules","requirement":"A landlord may not hold a deposit above one and one-half times one month's rent nor raise it over 10% a year; the interest belongs to the tenant and the deposit must be returned with an itemized list within 30 days.","key_value":"1.5 months' rent; yearly increase cap 10%","coverage_conditions":"All residential tenancies in New Jersey, including money held as prepaid rent; separate deposit rules apply to landlords of 10 or fewer units and 10 or more units.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Applies to residential rentals statewide"},"exemptions":"Security deposits for seasonal use or rental, meaning a term of not more than 125 consecutive days by a person whose permanent residence is elsewhere; farm worker housing is not treated as seasonal.","penalty":"Tenant may sue for double the amount wrongfully withheld plus court costs and reasonable attorney's fees; if the landlord fails to give required notice or pay interest, the tenant may apply the deposit plus 7% per annum interest to rent.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.2","quoted_span":"The maximum-security deposit to be collected by the landlord cannot be more than one and one-half times one month’s rent ( N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.2 ). It can be less. Any additional yearly security deposit increase may not exceed 10% of the current security deposit.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-01","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Safe Housing Act security deposit return after domestic violence termination","requirement":"When a tenant ends a lease under the Safe Housing Act, the landlord must make the security deposit plus interest available within 15 business days and notify the tenant within 3 business days where to collect it.","key_value":"15 business days","coverage_conditions":"Tenants who terminate a lease early under the Safe Housing Act because they or their child face an imminent threat of serious physical harm from a named person; not transient or seasonal rentals.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Domestic violence victims terminating a lease"},"exemptions":"Transient or seasonal rentals.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-9.6","quoted_span":"If a tenant terminates the lease agreement prior to the expiration of the lease pursuant to the Safe Housing Act , (N.J.S.A. 46:8-9.6), the tenant is entitled to the return of their security deposit. Within 15 business days after the lease is terminated, the landlord shall make available the return of the tenant’s security deposit, plus any interest earned, to the tenant or the tenant’s agent.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
